invariant: INV-26
file: aes_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh aes-equiv
reason: the AES-256 key expansion rotates the temporary of the step
  every second round key takes, where FIPS 197 applies SubWord alone. That
  step is the one AES-128 does not have, so only the AES-256 schedules
  change. bin/aes_equiv_test compares each schedule with the table's byte
  for byte, over keys whose second half is all that differs. Both
  architectures read the line, and the script builds that binary for each
  and runs it under qemu.

--- old
    return xor_block(prefix_xor(key), sub_last_word(first));
--- new
    return xor_block(prefix_xor(key), rot_sub_last_word(first));
